@meta
id: 20-06
title: Capital letters append
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-append
par: 44
minutes: 6
vscode: cut, scroll, paste, scroll, cut, scroll, paste, scroll
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
A lowercase register letter replaces what was there. The same letter in
uppercase *appends* to it. `"ayy` starts a collection; `"Ayy` adds the
next line to the end of it; `"ap` puts the whole collection out at once,
in the order you gathered it. That turns "move six lines from all over
the file to one place" from six round trips into one pass and one put.

The Unreleased section of this changelog mixes teams. Finance only
read the Payments section, so every `payments:` entry has to move out
of Unreleased and into Payments, below the entry already there, in the
same order they appear now. Move them; do not leave copies behind.

@keys
| `"add` | delete the line into `a`, replacing what was there |
| `"Add` | delete the line and append it to `a` |
| `"Ayy` | the same with yank, when the originals stay |
| `"ap` | put the whole collection |
| `n` | the next match, so you never scroll to find the next one |

@hints
Search for `payments:` and delete each match into the register with a
capital letter. The colon keeps the search away from the Payments
heading.
--
Because `a` starts empty, `"Add` works from the first line onward. In a
long session you would start with `"add` to throw away whatever `a` had
before.
--
`/payments:` Enter, `"Add`, then `n` `"Add` three more times. You end up
on the blank line above the Payments heading. `}` `}` goes to the blank
line after its one entry; `"aP` puts the four lines above that.

@solution
    /payments:<Enter> "Add n"Add n"Add n"Add  }} "aP  :wq<Enter>

The search does the finding, the capital letter does the collecting,
and there is exactly one put. Compare that to moving each line
separately: four searches, four deletes, four trips to the destination
and back, and the order of the trips has to be right or the entries
come out reversed.

Appending works with any register operation. `"Ayiw` adds a word to a
collection of words, and appending characterwise text to a linewise
register keeps the register linewise, which is usually what you want. It
is also the trick for editing a macro in chapter 21: put the register
out, fix the keystrokes, yank it back in.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## Unreleased

- orders: reject cancellation of shipped orders
- payments: retry a refund once on timeout
- customers: add `email` to the customer entity
- payments: surface `REFUND_FAILED` with the gateway response
- orders: record `cancellationReason` on the order
- payments: capture instead of authorise for card payments
- customers: validate the email format on create
- payments: drop the legacy `/v1/captures` call

## Payments

- payments: gateway client with retries

## 1.4.0

- orders: initial order provider
- customers: initial customer provider

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## Unreleased

- orders: reject cancellation of shipped orders
- customers: add `email` to the customer entity
- orders: record `cancellationReason` on the order
- customers: validate the email format on create

## Payments

- payments: gateway client with retries
- payments: retry a refund once on timeout
- payments: surface `REFUND_FAILED` with the gateway response
- payments: capture instead of authorise for card payments
- payments: drop the legacy `/v1/captures` call

## 1.4.0

- orders: initial order provider
- customers: initial customer provider
